validate/spectrum_diagnosis.log — running log of the diagnostic study of why the emulator with
loci = :spectrum puts k50 too high near the threshold (E6′: 25 inputs called alive that the
kernel called dead, 1 the other way; pooled x50 ratio 1.66). Exploratory study: hypotheses are
written here BEFORE each test; results are appended after. Script: validate/spectrum_diagnosis.jl.

== 2026-11-14, step 0: what is known before any new run ==

From the existing caches only (validate/emulator_spectrum_kernel.csv, _runs.csv; tabulated by a
scratch script, nothing new run): in the 25 discordant inputs (emulator alive, kernel dead) the
kernel's 40 runs die in a NARROW window per input (e.g. 177–238, 301–375, 230–268 generations
after the move; widest 263–867 and 674–1973), and in 23 of 25 none of 40 runs survives. So the
kernel's deaths look deterministic (a lag that runs away on a schedule), not a stochastic escape
from a metastable steady state. The emulator's trajectory for the same inputs ends alive with a
small, steady lag (lag_end 0.06–2.5 for most). Among the concordant dead inputs of the large-L
strata the emulator's median T is often later than the kernel's (e.g. 702 vs 323, 488 vs 175).

The chain: burn-in V_G → V_G during the move → lag → W̄ (Gaussian) → extinction given W̄.

Hypotheses to test (stated before step 1):
 H1 (burn-in): the kernel's V_G at the move is lower than the emulator's.
 H2 (V_G under the move): the kernel's V_G falls during the move below the emulator's
    (our working hypothesis, NOT established).
 H3 (response): the kernel's per-generation response of the mean genotype is smaller than the
    Gaussian V_G·λ/S given its own V_G, V_P and lag (non-Gaussian genotypes, skew).
 H4 (W̄): the kernel's realised mean fitness is lower than the Gaussian c·ω/√S·exp(−λ²/2S) given
    its own V_P and lag.
 H5 (extinction): given the kernel's W̄ trajectory, extinction_distribution's binomial hazard
    is right (deaths happen only where W̄ is so low that Binomial(N, W̄) < 2 is likely).
 H6 (deterministic vs stochastic): if the kernel's deaths came from runs escaping a steady
    state the emulator also has, the death times would be spread out; step 0 suggests not.

== step 1 (discovery set D + Dctl: 13 discordant E6′ inputs, odd positions in id order, and 8
   concordant controls, 2 per stratum-pair drawn by MersenneTwister(20261114); list in
   validate/spectrum_diagnosis_inputs.csv). Kernel: 10 runs per input, E6′'s seeds r = 1..10
   (KSEED + 100j + r), init = :equilibrium, burn-in 1000, i.e. E6′'s own runs re-run with
   extra statistics (no extra random draws). 196 runs, 165 s wall on 8 threads. ==

Measured (per-input detail: `analyse D+Dctl 10`, reproduced in spectrum_diagnosis.md):
 - H3 response: realised survivors' genotype shift / Gaussian V_G·λ/S (kernel's own V_G, V_P,
   λ, pooled over generations of the move) = 0.981–1.007 in all 21 inputs. REJECTED.
 - H4 W̄: realised mean fitness / Gaussian formula = 1.000 in all 21. REJECTED.
 - H5 extinction: the median W̄ in a run's last generation is 0.0013–0.0049 in every input with
   deaths (Binomial(1000, W̄) < 2 is then likely). H5 holds: the binomial link is right.
 - Substitution: the kernel's mean V_G trajectory (over runs alive) fed into the Gaussian lag
   recursion + extinction_distribution reproduces the kernel's median death time in every
   discordant input: e.g. 253 vs 244, 101 vs 99, 735 vs 720, 574 vs 544, 386 vs 374, 866 vs 534
   (the latter mean-over-alive biased), 848 vs 833, 1189 vs 1178; P2000 0 in all 12 with kernel
   deaths; for the reverse discordant input (06159, kernel alive) kernel V_G gives survival.
   The spectrum's own V_G in the same recursion reproduces the emulator (check of the recursion).
   => the whole bias is in V_G; the lag, W̄ and extinction links are right.
 - H1 burn-in: pre-move V_G kernel/spectrum = 0.45–0.98 (median ~0.76; 5000 loci 0.45–0.78).
   The kernel's GENIC variance at the move equals or exceeds the spectrum's V_G (0.38 vs 0.30,
   0.66 vs 0.63, 0.55 vs 0.54, 0.87 vs 0.78): the whole shortfall at the move is NEGATIVE LD
   (kernel LD/genic −0.03 to −0.64). The spectrum has no LD (V_G = genic). The multilocus
   haplotype moments (multilocus_equilibrium, drift = true) reproduce the kernel's LD for
   Input-12000 (VG 0.132, genic 0.416, ld −0.284 vs kernel 0.142, 0.379, −0.237).
 - But holding the kernel's pre-move V_G constant through the move gives P2000 = 1 in every
   discordant input: the lower V_G at the move is not enough by itself; the kernel's V_G must
   also FALL during the move (H2 holds): genic falls 25–90% within 250–500 generations, while
   the spectrum's V_G stays flat or rises.
 - H6: deaths are deterministic (narrow windows), confirmed.

Next hypothesis (H7, stated before the test): the spectrum's genic dynamics are too slow even
given the kernel's realised drivers. Test: drive the spectrum (from its equilibrium shape
scaled to each run's genic variance at −300) with the run's own lag, S = ω² + V_P and realised
survivors, generation by generation (no feedback), and compare genic(t)/genic(0) with the
kernel's. If the driven spectrum tracks the kernel, the bias is feedback from the missing LD
(H1) alone; if not, the genic dynamics themselves are wrong.
(Disclosure: the driven test had already been tried on 3 runs of Input-12000 while writing the
function; there the driven spectrum kept genic(+150)/genic(0) ≈ 0.96–1.0 against the kernel's 0.45–0.5.)

H7 result (driven test, 21 discovery inputs × 10 runs; genic(t)/genic(0), mean over runs alive):
 - In 12 of 13 discordant inputs the driven spectrum's genic variance falls later than the
   kernel's: +100 kernel 0.56–0.98 vs driven 0.88–1.55 (e.g. 0.67 vs 1.02, 0.74 vs 1.55,
   0.72 vs 0.97, 0.83 vs 1.00); +250 kernel 0.10–0.80 vs driven 0.16–1.11. By +500 the driven
   spectrum has often caught up or overshot (0.35 vs 0.33, 0.03 vs 0.13). In the house-of-cards-
   like inputs (00854, 07594, 01192, small V_G) the driven spectrum RISES at first (×1.2–1.5;
   the Barton–Turelli rise) while the kernel's genic variance falls.
 - Without the linked-selection term (linkage = :none) the driven spectrum hardly falls at all
   before +250: the decline it does have is Barton's linked-selection drift.
 - Controls (concordant, slower decline) agree to within a few % at +100/+250.
 => H7 holds: given the kernel's own lag and survivors, the spectrum's genic variance is too
   high in the first ~100–300 generations of the move, which is when the discordant kernel
   populations lose the race. Its decline comes from linked selection; the kernel's is earlier.
   LD at the move (H1) and this slow early decline both raise the emulator's V_G.

H8 (stated before the test): the linked-selection drift is too weak early in the move because
δ = 1/(2n) + V_G/S damps it (V_G/S is 0.1–0.6 in these inputs, far above 1/(2n) ≈ 5e-4), and/or
because b²V uses the genic rather than the (smaller) total V_G. Test: the driven spectrum with
δ = 1/(2n) (selection's part dropped, :dS0) and with the total V_G (:total), 4 runs per input.

H9 (stated before the test): the kernel's early V_G collapse and its negative LD are both
consequences of QTSim's tight linkage (at most one crossover per gamete). Prediction: with much
looser linkage the kernel's LD at the move is small, its genic variance does not collapse early,
and the discordant inputs survive (as the spectrum predicts). Test: VARIANT = "many"
(CrossoverModel = :all, RecombPoissonMean = 20: every one of Poisson(20) points applied), the 13
discordant discovery inputs, 6 runs each (same seeds), and the spectrum emulator with the same
Params (its pair classes then those of the looser linkage). A pilot with free recombination
(Poisson((L−1)/2) points; 382 s per run, too slow) on Input-14110 survived to +2000 (1 run;
all 40 E6′ runs with QTSim's crossover died); multilocus_equilibrium there gives LD −0.017
against −0.08 with QTSim's crossover. A pilot "many" run on Input-14110 also survived (1 run).

k50 on six discovery inputs (`k50` mode; kernel: 10 runs at each of ×0.25/0.35/0.5/0.7/1.0 the
input's rate, fresh seeds 50261114000+…, x50 by log-linear interpolation of the fraction alive;
the grid only resolves k50 to about ±17%):
  input                emulator/kernel k50
  QT0500-3/04943       0.0849/0.0406 = 2.09
  QT1000-3/09697       0.1221/0.0770 = 1.59
  QT1000-5/14110       0.0534/0.0365 = 1.46
  QT5000-3/12000       0.1973/0.0577 = 3.42
  QT5000-5/12463       0.0781/0.0439 = 1.78
  QT5000-5/01925       0.0464/0.0376 = 1.23
The decomposition with V_G held constant (Gaussian recursion at the spectrum's or the kernel's
pre-move V_G) is not informative about shares: with V_G constant k50 is 4–10× the actual one
for both, i.e. V_G's fall during the move dominates k50 for BOTH models; the pre-move level
alone (constant spectrum vs constant kernel V_G) moves k50 by ×1.02–1.89 (log 0.02–0.64), of a
total log(E/K) of 0.21–1.23. Shares are instead measured with the prototype below.

H8 result (driven, 4 runs per discovery input; genic(t)/genic(0)):
 - δ = 1/(2n) (selection's part of δ dropped): the driven spectrum's genic variance collapses far
   too fast (+100: 0.01–0.55 where the kernel has 0.57–0.98; controls too: 0.27–0.99 vs 0.88–1.03).
 - total V_G instead of genic in b²V and δ: almost no change (+100 1.00 vs 1.00; 0.85 vs 0.97).
 => the kernel lies between δ = 1/(2n) + V_G/S (too slow) and δ = 1/(2n) (much too fast): the
   linked-selection drift is the right mechanism with the association decay δ overestimated.
   This is the open problem noted in CLAUDE.md. Exploratory next step (a FIT, so any claim
   needs other inputs): δ = 1/(2n) + d·V_G/S for d = 0.5, 0.25, 0.1 on the discovery inputs.

Prototype P1 (exploratory; src/spectrum.jl keyword `ldratio`, default 1 = unchanged, tests in
test/closures.jl): the spectrum with a constant LD factor r = V_G/genic from the multilocus
haplotype moments at the fixed optimum with drift (multilocus_equilibrium(p; drift = true,
loci = :multilocus); nothing fitted): the lowering's V_G is r × genic, and every mutation's
directional selection is r·aλ/S (so the response is r × the genic one).
Discovery result (the six k50 inputs above, all from D): r model 0.32–0.75 vs kernel's measured
pre-move V_G/genic 0.37–0.79 (the model's LD is 5–25% stronger); emulator/kernel k50
  2.09 → 0.93, 1.59 → 1.06, 1.46 → 0.80, 3.42 → 1.50, 1.78 → 1.10, 1.23 → 0.99;
geometric mean 1.82 → 1.04 (mean log 0.599 → 0.042): on these inputs the missing LD accounts for
~93% of the log k50 bias. Caveat: the model's r is lower than the kernel's, which offsets the
too-slow early genic decline found by H7, so two errors partly cancel.

H10 (stated before running it): on the E6′ inputs NOT used for discovery (sets C, Cctl and the
unselected concordant ones; 139 inputs, the kernel's 40 E6′ runs each, already cached), P1
reduces the discordant calls (the spectrum has 13 alive/dead + 0 dead/alive in C, and errors
among the 139 in all) and brings the pooled x50 ratio (1.66 on all 160 for the spectrum) into
[0.8, 1.25]. Not independent of E6′'s aggregate results (seen), but the prototype was not
developed on these inputs.

H11 (fresh confirmation, stated before selecting or running anything): 64 new published inputs
(8 per stratum, MersenneTwister(20261119) permutation of the inputs not excluded by
emulator_spectrum.jl, not in its E1–E4 sample, its E6′ pool (1200) or E6′, with MoveFitnessSD/kc
in [0.3, 3], E6′'s prefilter), 20 kernel runs each as E6′ (init = :equilibrium, burn-in 1000;
seeds 60261114000 + 100j + r). Prediction: the spectrum emulator calls more inputs alive that
the kernel calls dead than the reverse, with pooled x50 ratio > 1.25; P1 has fewer
discordant calls and a pooled ratio in [0.8, 1.25]. Exploratory; no verdict.

δ grid result (driven, 4 runs per discovery input): no single weight d in δ = 1/(2n) + d·V_G/S
reproduces the kernel's genic trajectory. At +100 the kernel has already lost 5–30% while the
driven spectrum with d = 0.5 has lost ≤ 10% (or risen: 00854 1.26, 01192 1.27); by +250 d = 0.5
is about right in many inputs; by +500 every d ≤ 0.5 overshoots (e.g. 14110 0.15 vs 0.02, 07594
0.21 vs 0.04, 06159 0.75 vs 0.43). d = 0.25 or 0.1 overshoot earlier. So the kernel's decline
is EARLIER and more GRADUAL than Barton's term gives: the shape, not only the scale, differs.
Barton's term grows as b² = (λ/S)² and so starts weakly while the lag is small, whereas the
kernel's genic variance starts falling at once. Candidate causes not tested here: (a) the
kernel's allele spectrum at the move holds more intermediate-frequency variance than the
spectrum's equilibrium shape (its genic variance is 0–25% above the spectrum's V_G in most
discordant inputs), which directional selection sweeps out first; (b) linked selection under
stabilising selection already present before the move (background-selection-like; the
spectrum's term is zero at λ = 0); (c) multiple alleles per locus (continuum of alleles vs
infinite sites). No δ prototype is pursued: fitting d would be a fit to these inputs and does
not fix the shape.

H9 result: with looser linkage (VARIANT many: Poisson(20) crossovers, all applied) every one of
the 78 runs (13 discordant discovery inputs × 6) is alive at +2000, where with QTSim's single
crossover 128 of 130 E6′ runs of the same inputs were dead; the kernel's pre-move V_G/genic is
0.71–0.99 (QTSim crossover 0.37–0.79), and its V_G no longer collapses (lag at +1999 within
1.0–1.8× the spectrum's, flat). The spectrum emulator (same Params) also has them alive. H9
HOLDS: the discordance is a linkage effect, through the negative LD that tight linkage lets
stabilising selection build (missing from the spectrum) and the early genic collapse (too slow
in the spectrum).

H10 result (139 E6′ inputs not used for discovery): spectrum 13 alive/kernel dead, 0 reverse,
accuracy 0.906, pooled x50 ratio 1.361; P1 (spectrum + ldratio) 0 alive/kernel dead, 13 reverse,
accuracy 0.906, ratio 0.798. P1 removes the bias's direction but OVERCORRECTS (ratio just
below 0.8; new errors in the 100- and 500-locus strata, where the spectrum had none or few).
H10 fails as stated (discordance not reduced; ratio at the lower edge of [0.8, 1.25]).

LD model vs kernel (42 diagnostic inputs): r_model = V_G/genic of multilocus_equilibrium is
BELOW the kernel's measured pre-move V_G/genic in 39 of 42 (differences up to 0.23, e.g.
0.54 vs 0.77, 0.30 vs 0.42; within 0.02 where LD is weak): the multilocus moments make the
negative LD 10–40% too strong.

Diagnostic P1k (spectrum + ldratio = the kernel's own measured r; needs kernel runs, so not a
predictor): of the 25 discordant inputs in D and C, P1k still calls 7 alive (C: 03416, 14301,
14872; D: 01192, 00854, 12000, 12463) and fixes 18; it adds no wrong "dead" call beyond the
reverse case 06159 (wrong in every variant) and Cctl 05035 (f 0.90, P1 0). P1 with the model's
r fixes all 25 (and errs on 05035 and 06159). Reading: with the right LD level ~70% of the
discordant calls are explained by the missing LD; the rest is the too-slow genic decline (H7),
which the model's too-strong LD happens to compensate in these inputs and overcompensates in
others (H10: ratio 0.80, 13 errors the other way).
Tests after the src keyword: closures 139/139, emulator 86/86, theory 68/68.

k50 share (six k50 inputs, spectrum + ldratio = kernel's measured r; same kernel k50s):
  ratios 1.17, 1.15, 0.96, 1.71, 1.25, 1.02 (spectrum 2.09, 1.59, 1.46, 3.42, 1.78, 1.23;
  P1 with the model's r 0.93, 1.06, 0.80, 1.50, 1.10, 0.99).
  Mean log ratio: spectrum 0.599 (×1.82); with the kernel's LD level 0.173 (×1.19); with the
  model's LD level 0.042 (×1.04).
  => the missing LD explains ~71% of the log k50 bias on these inputs (0.426 of 0.599) and the
  too-slow early genic decline the remaining ~29% (0.173); the model's stronger LD covers that
  remainder here by compensation. Kernel k50 resolution about ±17% (rate grid), so these
  shares are rough.

H11 result (fresh: 64 new inputs, 20 kernel runs each; 1280 runs, ~7 min wall on 7 threads):
 kernel fraction alive mean 0.152; inputs with none alive 54, all 9, some 1 (the sample is
 dominated by fast rates, so x50 is poorly determined).
 spectrum:          5 alive/kernel dead, 1 dead/kernel alive, accuracy 0.906, mean |f − P2000|
                    0.083, pooled x50 ratio 0.935
 P1 (model ldratio): 0 alive/kernel dead, 1 dead/kernel alive, accuracy 0.984, mean |f − P2000|
                    0.007, pooled x50 ratio 0.954
 => the direction of the spectrum's errors is confirmed on fresh inputs (5 vs 1), but the
 predicted pooled ratio > 1.25 is NOT seen (0.935: few inputs near the threshold); P1 has
 fewer discordant calls here (1 vs 6). Together with H10 (139 E6′ inputs: P1 overcorrects,
 ratio 0.80, 13 errors the other way) the prototype is not established: it moves the
 threshold by about the right amount on average in large-L inputs and too far in small-L ones.

